## li-shak | Shakespeare | lit | 0 | - | li-phys
story 4 | We don't know how Shakespeare spelled his own name
- His six surviving signatures spell it several different ways, none of them "Shakespeare" exactly as we write it.
- Spelling simply wasn't standardised in Elizabethan England.
- *Only a handful of documents in his own hand survive at all.*
> Hamlet is his longest play

quiz 3 | Which is Shakespeare's longest play?
* Hamlet
o King Lear
o Macbeth
o Othello
= Hamlet runs to over 4,000 lines in its fullest version: about four hours uncut.
= Macbeth is one of his shortest.
> Actors have a superstition about Macbeth

story 3 | Why actors say "the Scottish play"
- Theatre tradition holds that saying "Macbeth" inside a theatre brings disaster.
- The cure, by legend: leave the theatre, spin around three times, spit, and knock to be let back in.
- *The superstition seems to date from the late 1800s, not Shakespeare's time.*
> His plays were performed in a theatre that burned down

story 4 | The Globe burned down during Henry VIII
- 1613: a stage cannon fired during a performance set the thatched roof alight.
- The theatre burned to the ground in under two hours. One man's breeches caught fire; someone put it out with ale.
- *The modern reconstruction opened in 1997, near the original site.*
> Iambic pentameter is the heartbeat of his verse

explain 3 | Iambic pentameter
- Five pairs of syllables, each unstressed then stressed: da-DUM da-DUM da-DUM da-DUM da-DUM.
- "But SOFT, what LIGHT through YONder WINdow BREAKS?"
- *Characters often drop into prose when they're low-born, mad, or joking.*
> Some of his famous lines are misquoted

myth 3 F | Juliet's "Wherefore art thou Romeo?" means "Where are you, Romeo?"
= "Wherefore" means "why". She's asking why he has to be a Montague, her family's enemy.
= The next lines: "Deny thy father and refuse thy name."
> Next: the Greeks

## li-greek | Epics and tragedy | lit | 0 | - | li-shak
explain 3 | The Iliad covers only a few weeks of a ten-year war
- It starts in the war's tenth year, with Achilles' rage at Agamemnon.
- It ends with Hector's funeral, before the wooden horse.
- *The Trojan Horse barely appears in Homer:* it's mentioned in the Odyssey and told fully in Virgil's Aeneid.
> The Odyssey took ten more years to get home

quiz 3 | What did Odysseus tell the Cyclops his name was?
* Nobody
o Odysseus
o Zeus
o Achilles
= When the blinded Polyphemus shouted for help, he said "Nobody is killing me." No one came.
> Greek tragedy was a competition

explain 3 | Greek tragedy was a competition with prizes
- At Athens' Dionysia festival, playwrights each presented three tragedies and a satyr play.
- Sophocles reportedly won about 20 times.
- *Only a few dozen complete Greek tragedies survive, out of hundreds performed.*
> Oedipus is the original plot twist

explain 4 | Dramatic irony in Oedipus Rex
- Oedipus hunts the killer of the old king to lift a plague from Thebes.
- The audience knows from the start that he is the killer, and that the king was his father.
- *Every confident line he speaks lands differently for them.*
> Aristotle wrote the first guide to plots

explain 3 | Aristotle's Poetics: plot first
- A good tragedy has a beginning, middle and end, and a reversal of fortune.
- Catharsis: the audience's pity and fear are purged.
- *Screenwriting guides still quote it.*
> Next: the great Russians

## li-russ | Russian novels | lit | 0 | - | li-greek
explain 4 | War and Peace isn't just a novel
- Tolstoy's book follows Russian families through Napoleon's invasion of 1812.
- It breaks off into long essays on history and free will.
- Tolstoy said it was "not a novel, even less is it a poem, and still less a historical chronicle."
> Anna Karenina's first line is famous

quiz 3 | Complete Tolstoy's opening: "Happy families are all alike; every unhappy family…"
* "…is unhappy in its own way."
o "…has a secret."
o "…ends in tragedy."
o "…is like every other."
= The opening of Anna Karenina (1877).
= Researchers have since named an "Anna Karenina principle": success needs many things going right; failure needs only one going wrong.
> Dostoevsky wrote to pay gambling debts

story 4 | The Gambler was written in 26 days to beat a deadline
- Dostoevsky had signed a contract that would hand his future rights to a publisher if he missed a deadline.
- He hired a stenographer, Anna Snitkina, and dictated The Gambler in under a month.
- *They married the next year.*
> Crime and Punishment is told from inside a murderer's head

explain 4 | Raskolnikov's theory
- A poor ex-student argues that extraordinary people may break moral laws for a greater good.
- He murders a pawnbroker to test it, then unravels from guilt and fever.
- *The novel is less a whodunit than a "why-and-what-then".*
> Chekhov revolutionised the short story

explain 3 | Chekhov's quiet endings
- Chekhov's stories often end without resolution: characters simply carry on.
- He was a practising doctor, and joked that medicine was his wife and literature his mistress.
- *Modern short fiction owes him its ambiguity.*
> Next: Victorian novels

## li-vic | The 19th-century English novel | lit | 0 | - | li-russ
story 4 | Dickens published in instalments, like a TV series
- His novels came out in monthly or weekly parts, with cliffhangers to sell the next issue.
- A popular story says New York crowds at the docks shouted for news of whether Little Nell had died.
- *Serial fiction invented binge-reading's cliffhanger 150 years early.*
> Austen published anonymously

story 3 | Jane Austen's novels said "By a Lady"
- Sense and Sensibility (1811) was credited only to "A Lady".
- She earned modest sums in her lifetime; her name only appeared publicly after her death in 1817.
- *She's now on the Bank of England £10 note.*
> The Brontës used male pen names

quiz 3 | What pen names did Charlotte, Emily and Anne Brontë first publish under?
* Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell
o George Eliot
o Boz, Phiz and Bell
o J., E. and A. Brown
= They kept their initials but used ambiguous names, partly to avoid prejudice against women writers.
= George Eliot was Mary Ann Evans, a different novelist.
> Middlemarch is often called the greatest English novel

explain 4 | Middlemarch
- George Eliot's 1871–72 novel follows a provincial town through marriages, reform and failed ambitions.
- Its last line praises "the number who lived faithfully a hidden life, and rest in unvisited tombs."
- *Virginia Woolf called it "one of the few English novels written for grown-up people".*
> Next: modernism breaks the rules

## li-mod | Modernism | lit | 0 | - | li-vic
explain 4 | Ulysses happens in one day
- Joyce's novel follows Leopold Bloom around Dublin on 16 June 1904.
- Each chapter uses a different style, loosely mapped onto Homer's Odyssey.
- *Fans celebrate 16 June every year as Bloomsday.*
> Stream of consciousness

explain 3 | Stream of consciousness
- The narrative follows a character's thoughts as they flow: fragments, memories, associations.
- Virginia Woolf's Mrs Dalloway takes place over a single day in London, moving between minds.
- *It tries to capture how thinking actually feels.*
> The Waste Land was edited heavily by a friend

story 4 | Ezra Pound cut The Waste Land nearly in half
- T. S. Eliot's 1922 poem was much longer in draft.
- Pound slashed whole sections; Eliot dedicated the poem to him as "il miglior fabbro" (the better craftsman).
- *The draft manuscripts were rediscovered in 1968.*
> Kafka's opening line is one of literature's most famous

quiz 3 | What does Gregor Samsa wake up as in Kafka's The Metamorphosis?
* A monstrous insect (vermin)
o A cockroach, specifically
o A beetle, as Kafka drew it on the cover
o A rat
= Kafka's word "Ungeziefer" means vermin; the species is never named.
= Kafka told his publisher the insect must not be drawn on the cover.
> Next: dystopias

## li-dys | Dystopias | lit | 0 | - | li-mod
explain 4 | Orwell's 1984 and Newspeak
- The Party shrinks the language so that dissent becomes literally unthinkable.
- "Doublethink": holding two contradictory beliefs and accepting both.
- *Orwell wrote much of it on the remote Scottish island of Jura while seriously ill with tuberculosis.*
> Huxley imagined control by pleasure instead

explain 4 | Brave New World: control through comfort
- Huxley's 1932 society keeps people docile with engineered happiness, a drug called soma and endless entertainment.
- No one needs banning books when no one wants to read them.
- *Neil Postman argued in 1985 that Huxley's vision was the more accurate one.*
> Bradbury's firemen start fires

quiz 3 | In Fahrenheit 451, what is 451?
* The temperature at which paper burns, in °F (as Bradbury had it)
o The year it's set
o The number of banned books
o A street address
= Bradbury used 451°F as the ignition point of paper. Real figures vary.
> Zamyatin came first

story 4 | We: the dystopia that inspired Orwell
- Yevgeny Zamyatin's We (1920s) depicts citizens with numbers instead of names, living in glass houses.
- It was banned in the Soviet Union.
- *Orwell reviewed it in 1946, three years before 1984 was published.*
> Next: poetry

## li-poem | Poetry forms | lit | 0 | - | li-dys
explain 3 | The sonnet: 14 lines and a turn
- Petrarchan: 8 lines setting a problem, 6 answering it.
- Shakespearean: three quatrains and a closing couplet with a twist.
- *The "volta" (turn) is where the poem changes direction.*
> Haiku in English often miss the point

myth 3 F | A haiku is defined by having 5-7-5 syllables.
= Japanese haiku count "on" (sound units), which don't match English syllables.
= Traditionally they also include a seasonal reference (kigo) and a "cutting word" contrasting two images.
> Free verse dropped rhyme and metre

quiz 3 | Who wrote "Do not go gentle into that good night"?
* Dylan Thomas
o T. S. Eliot
o W. B. Yeats
o Philip Larkin
= A villanelle, written for his dying father: only two rhymes and two refrains across 19 lines.
> Emily Dickinson published almost nothing

story 4 | Emily Dickinson's 1,800 hidden poems
- Fewer than a dozen of her poems were published in her lifetime, mostly anonymously.
- After her death in 1886, her sister found nearly 1,800 in her room, many sewn into little booklets.
- *Her dashes were "corrected" by early editors. Modern editions restore them.*
> Next: science fiction

## li-sf | Science fiction | lit | 0 | - | li-poem
story 4 | Jules Verne's moon cannon launched from Florida
- From the Earth to the Moon (1865) fires three men from a giant cannon in Florida.
- Apollo 11 launched from Florida a century later, with three crew, and splashed down in the Pacific, as Verne's capsule did.
- *A cannon launch would have flattened them, though.*
> H. G. Wells imagined the atom bomb

story 5 | H. G. Wells coined "atomic bombs" in 1914
- In The World Set Free, Wells described bombs using radioactivity that kept exploding for days.
- Physicist Leó Szilárd read it in 1932. The next year he conceived the nuclear chain reaction.
- *Szilárd later drafted the letter Einstein signed warning Roosevelt.*
> Asimov's three laws of robotics

explain 3 | Asimov's Three Laws of Robotics
- 1. A robot may not injure a human or, through inaction, allow a human to come to harm.
- 2. It must obey humans, unless that conflicts with law 1. 3. It must protect itself, unless that conflicts with 1 or 2.
- *Most of his robot stories are about the laws going wrong.*
> Ursula K. Le Guin rewrote gender

explain 4 | The Left Hand of Darkness
- Le Guin's 1969 novel is set on a planet whose people have no fixed sex.
- A human envoy struggles to see them as people rather than as "men" or "women".
- *It won both the Hugo and Nebula awards.*
> Clarke's three laws

quiz 3 | Complete Arthur C. Clarke's third law: "Any sufficiently advanced technology is…"
* "…indistinguishable from magic."
o "…dangerous."
o "…inevitable."
o "…invisible."
= From his 1973 revision of Profiles of the Future.
> Next: how stories are built

## li-craft | Writing craft | lit | 0 | - | li-sf
explain 3 | Show, don't tell
- Telling: "She was nervous." Showing: "She checked the door was locked. Then checked it again."
- Showing lets readers infer, which makes them feel it.
- *Chekhov: "Don't tell me the moon is shining; show me the glint of light on broken glass."* (A paraphrase of his letters.)
> Point of view changes everything

explain 3 | Unreliable narrators
- A narrator who misleads you, or themselves.
- Ishiguro's The Remains of the Day: a butler whose polished narration hides what he's lost.
- *The story is in the gap between what's said and what's true.*
> In medias res

explain 3 | Start in the middle
- "In medias res": begin in the middle of the action, fill in the past later.
- Homer did it: the Odyssey opens with Odysseus already stranded for years.
- *Every good card in this feed tries to do the same.*
> The hero's journey is an influential template

explain 3 | The hero's journey
- Joseph Campbell (1949): myths across cultures share a pattern of departure, trials, transformation and return.
- George Lucas credited Campbell's work as an influence on Star Wars.
- *Critics argue it's been applied too rigidly.*
> Next: Latin American literature

## li-latam | Latin American literature | lit | 0 | - | li-craft
explain 4 | Magical realism
- Gabriel García Márquez's One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967) mixes the miraculous with the everyday, without comment.
- A woman ascends to heaven while folding sheets. A rain of yellow flowers falls.
- *He said he learned the tone from his grandmother's deadpan storytelling.*
> Borges wrote stories like philosophy puzzles

explain 4 | Borges: Pierre Menard
- A French writer sets out to write Don Quixote, word for word, not by copying but by becoming someone who would write it.
- Borges's narrator claims Menard's identical text is richer, because its context is different.
- *A 1939 short story that anticipated decades of literary theory.*
> Borges was director of Argentina's National Library while going blind

story 3 | Borges ran a library he couldn't read
- In 1955 Borges became director of Argentina's National Library, just as his eyesight failed completely.
- He wrote of God's irony in giving him "books and the night" at the same time.
> Next: maths in literature

## li-math | Maths in literature | lit | 0 | - | li-latam
story 4 | Lewis Carroll was a maths don
- Charles Dodgson lectured in mathematics at Christ Church, Oxford, and wrote on logic and determinants.
- Alice in Wonderland is full of logic games and nonsense that's carefully exact.
- *The Mad Hatter's "I see what I eat" vs "I eat what I see" is a lesson in converse statements.*
> Calvino's Invisible Cities is built like a mathematical structure

explain 4 | The Oulipo: writing with constraints
- A French group of writers and mathematicians founded in 1960.
- Georges Perec wrote a 300-page novel, La Disparition, without the letter "e".
- *Constraints, they argued, spark creativity rather than limit it.*
> Gravity's Rainbow follows a rocket

explain 4 | Gravity's Rainbow and the V-2
- Thomas Pynchon's 1973 novel revolves around the V-2 rocket, whose parabolic flight shapes the book.
- A character's sexual encounters seem to predict where rockets will land in London.
- *The Pulitzer board refused the fiction jury's recommendation to award it the prize.*
> Literature track complete
